1
00:00:00,300 --> 00:00:01,397
Thirty robots.

2
00:00:01,447 --> 00:00:03,118
One warehouse floor.

3
00:00:03,168 --> 00:00:09,429
And no central computer telling any of them what to do.

4
00:00:09,479 --> 00:00:11,150
This is ROBOTON.

5
00:00:11,700 --> 00:00:15,902
Today, warehouse robots depend on a single fleet server.

6
00:00:15,953 --> 00:00:19,210
When it fails, the whole floor stops.

7
00:00:19,260 --> 00:00:24,408
In narrow single-lane aisles, robots meet head-on, and collide or deadlock.

8
00:00:24,458 --> 00:00:30,550
And every robot you add makes the central planner's job harder, not easier.

9
00:00:31,100 --> 00:00:34,419
ROBOTON moves every decision onto the robots themselves.

10
00:00:34,469 --> 00:00:44,106
Each AMR plans its own route, bids for its own work, and negotiates right of way with its neighbours over a peer-to-peer mesh.

11
00:00:44,156 --> 00:00:52,950
This is our live floor: thirty AMRs, fifty pick points in the aisles, six drops, thirty charging bays, and no supervisor.

12
00:00:53,500 --> 00:00:58,159
An order enters through the gateway, which only announces it.

13
00:00:58,209 --> 00:01:00,514
It never picks a robot.

14
00:01:00,564 --> 00:01:11,815
Every AMR prices the job for itself: the extra travel it adds, a low-battery penalty, minus credit for how long the order has waited.

15
00:01:11,865 --> 00:01:13,699
The lowest bid wins.

16
00:01:13,749 --> 00:01:19,350
Only nearby zones bid, so each auction costs about thirty-four radio frames.

17
00:01:19,900 --> 00:01:31,037
Before it moves, each AMR books its whole route as time windows on every junction and aisle, checked against the plans its peers broadcast.

18
00:01:31,087 --> 00:01:38,961
Then it drives by order, not by clock: it enters only after everyone booked ahead has left.

19
00:01:39,011 --> 00:01:44,089
Cyan robots are yielding; the arcs show who waits for whom.

20
00:01:44,139 --> 00:01:47,818
When two plans clash, the earlier commitment stands.

21
00:01:47,868 --> 00:01:48,750
No referee.

22
00:01:49,300 --> 00:01:53,807
Every aisle is a single lane, driven in either direction.

23
00:01:53,857 --> 00:02:01,099
A robot owns an aisle from junction to junction, so two robots can never meet head-on.

24
00:02:01,149 --> 00:02:05,200
Here, ours waits its turn behind the robot ahead.

25
00:02:05,250 --> 00:02:15,226
Orders are picked right in the aisle; the pick light marks the shelf, and the robot holds that stretch while it loads.

26
00:02:15,276 --> 00:02:21,150
And each AMR carries one carton, delivering it before it collects the next.

27
00:02:21,700 --> 00:02:29,487
We tested against a classic stop-and-wait baseline: same floor, same hundred and twenty orders, same seed.

28
00:02:29,537 --> 00:02:35,855
The baseline logged two hundred and six collisions and jammed after forty-seven orders.

29
00:02:35,905 --> 00:02:45,162
ROBOTON delivered all one hundred and twenty, with zero collisions, and the same held on every seed we compared.

30
00:02:45,212 --> 00:02:50,550
Across twelve randomized thirty-robot runs and forty benchmark runs: zero collisions.

31
00:02:51,100 --> 00:02:54,915
Under the hood, it is built to survive failure.

32
00:02:54,965 --> 00:02:59,638
Low battery raises a robot's bid and sends it to charge.

33
00:02:59,688 --> 00:03:03,932
If a robot fails, its orders return to the mesh.

34
00:03:03,982 --> 00:03:12,950
Plans travel as compact radio frames, the dashboard only watches, and six hundred and fifty-six passing tests pin every safety rule.

35
00:03:13,500 --> 00:03:15,524
No central planner.

36
00:03:15,574 --> 00:03:16,907
No dispatcher.

37
00:03:16,957 --> 00:03:20,363
No single point of failure.

38
00:03:20,413 --> 00:03:26,585
Just a fleet that coordinates itself, safely, at scale.

39
00:03:26,635 --> 00:03:27,276
ROBOTON.

40
00:03:27,326 --> 00:03:29,350
The fleet decides.
